# A nineteen-metre charter boat, floating.
#
# **This scene exists to answer one question: does it sit upright and stay
# there.** Not whether it looks right — `assets/meshes/jib_vi_wide.obj` was
# judged on that separately — but whether the buoyancy model this engine has
# can hold a hull-shaped object level for minutes without capsizing, and
# whether it rights itself when pushed.
#
# **The pontoons are hand-placed and that is the whole point of the scene.**
# `Buoyancy`'s default is four pontoons derived from the node's own size, at
# its horizontal corners, with radii summing to the body's volume. For a crate
# that is exactly right. For a boat it is badly wrong, because the node's
# bounding box is 19.07 x 9.96 x 6.60 m and **most of that box is superstructure
# and air** — the hull is the bottom 1.66 m of it. Four corner spheres sized to
# the whole box would displace several times what the hull does and the boat
# would ride on the surface like a cork, which is the failure the component's
# own documentation warns about in the neighbouring case.
#
# So twelve pontoons, along the hull, tapering with the waterlines:
#
#   - **Twelve, not four**, because righting torque comes from the forces
#     landing at *different places*. Four give a boat almost no roll stiffness
#     at the beam, and a hull with no roll stiffness lies down at the first
#     asymmetry and never gets up — which is exactly the "capsizes for no
#     reason" this scene is built to catch.
#   - **z = ±1.951 amidships narrowing to ±0.918 at the bow**, because the hull
#     narrows. Pontoons wider than the hull would generate a righting moment
#     the boat does not have, which is the same lie as the cork, told the other
#     way round.
#   - **Centred at y = -0.247 with radius 1.093**, so each sits exactly two
#     thirds submerged at rest. That is deliberate: a sphere fully under
#     displaces a constant volume no matter how the boat moves, and contributes
#     **nothing** to stability. A sphere crossing the surface gains and loses
#     displacement as it rises and falls, and that change is the restoring
#     force. Pontoons buried below the waterline are a common way to build a
#     boat that floats and still capsizes.
#
# **The hull is `jib_vi_wide.obj`, and the beam is why every number here moved.**
# The first hull had a cabin ±2.52 m standing on a deck ±2.41 m — wider than the
# deck it sat on — so a player could stand on the aft deck or the foredeck and
# could not walk between them. Beam went 5.75 -> 6.60 m and the cabin half-width
# 2.52 -> 1.47, which buys a continuous side deck of 1.250 m clear at its
# narrowest, both sides, bow to stern. That is a gameplay requirement, but it is
# also the more faithful boat: a party boat sells rail space, and the reference
# photograph has anglers lined along the sides.
#
# Both pontoon numbers are solved for, not chosen. The wide hull displaces
# 57.64 m^3 below the waterline against the narrow one's 50.0, a ratio of 1.152,
# so `mass` scales 38000 -> **43776** and each of the twelve carries 3.648 m^3.
# Asking that to be two thirds of a sphere gives a whole sphere of 5.472 m^3 and
# therefore r = 1.0932. The offset follows from the spherical cap: a centre
# 0.247 m below the surface leaves a submerged depth of h = r + 0.247 = 1.340,
# and pi*h^2*(3r - h)/3 = 3.648 m^3 — two thirds again, which is the point.
# Total 12 x 3.648 = 43.78 m^3 puts the waterline at y = 0, where the model's
# origin already is, because it was exported that way. The z offsets scale with
# the beam by the same 6.60/5.75 = 1.1478, and the collider's half-beam with
# them, 2.87 -> 3.30.
#
# **The scene first shipped with r = 0.945 at y = -0.6, and that is the failure
# the bullet above describes, written into the file it warns in.** Those
# displace the same 38.7 m^3 — the flotation arithmetic was right — but the
# spheres are **91% submerged**, leaving 11.6% reserve buoyancy, so they
# saturate almost immediately and stop generating any restoring force. The
# difference is not subtle and is measured below.
#
# **The wind is light on purpose.** A real sea would make a capsize ambiguous:
# a boat knocked over by a wave is a boat, a boat that lies down on flat water
# is a bug. `speed = 3.5` gives a surface with enough texture to see the hull
# respond without ever being the cause. The harder wave case belongs in its own
# scene once this one holds.
#
# The water is `simulation = "deterministic"` — the height-field tier, which
# carries force, the sim hash and `--assert` (ADR 0059 §1). A cinematic volume
# around a nineteen-metre hull is a separate experiment and a separate scene.
#
# **What this hull is measured to do.** Dropped from 0.4 m it settles at a
# mean of y = +0.024 — 2.4 cm above the designed line — and is still there at
# tick 36000, ten minutes in, having drifted 11 mm and bobbing 3.2–4.1 cm peak
# to peak on the wave texture. The 2.4 cm is not this scene's arithmetic: it is
# a 2.5% buoyancy excess the engine adds whenever `damp_linear` is nonzero
# (the fixed step applies gravity before buoyancy, and the displacement-scaled
# damping rectifies that jitter into net lift — measured at the same 2.5% on an
# independent four-sphere calibration body, and extrapolating damp_linear -> 0
# lands on y = 0 exactly). Do not tune `mass` to cancel it; the tune would go
# wrong the moment the integrator is fixed. Total tilt off vertical at rest is
# under 0.7°, so it is level in trim as well as in heel. Heeled and released
# **from the level settle height** it rights itself from 80° and inverts from
# 82° — but that release condition flatters it: released at each angle's own
# heeled equilibrium the vanishing angle is **~75°**, and a static GZ curve
# computed outside the physics engine from the same spherical-cap formula
# agrees at 74.9° (GZ peaks at 0.70 m at 21°, GM = 2.7 m). Quote 75°, and say
# which release you mean if this line ever becomes an assertion. On the
# pontoons this scene first shipped with, the same test rights from 40° and
# inverts from 42°. Once over, it floats hull-up indefinitely — the inverted
# state is stable, which is correct for an enclosed superstructure.
#
# **Having an angle of vanishing stability is not the bug — every real boat has
# one.** Coming back from 30° and not from 60° would be correct behaviour. The
# defect was having it at 40°, which is inside the range an ordinary sea
# reaches, and it came entirely from the two numbers above.
#
# The wind sweep says the same thing from the other side, re-measured on this
# hull: at `speed = 40` — the schema takes 40 and refuses 41 — the sea it
# builds swings the boat through 35 m peak to peak and rolls it 10.8° at
# worst, and it still never goes over; at 35 m/s that is 27 m and 6.1°. (The
# 17 m and "under 8°" this paragraph used to quote were the narrow hull's and
# are void.) On this tier there is no wind speed a scene can author that
# capsizes it — and part of the reason is that `loom_physics` has no windage
# term at all: wind reaches the hull only as wave slope, so there is no
# beam-on heeling force here at any speed.
#
# Reproducing that costs a workaround worth knowing: `loom sim --assert` reads
# x, y and z on a node and has **no orientation axis at all**. Roll is read by
# parenting a marker to the Boat at local (0, 0, 5) — under a rotation phi
# about X it lands at (0, -5 sin phi, 5 cos phi), so `atan2(-dy, dz)` recovers
# a signed angle over the full ±180° and can tell "righted" from "inverted",
# which a mast's tilt from vertical cannot. The markers belong in a throwaway
# copy, not in here.
#
# One reading that surprises: `events.submerged` fires **once**, in the drop
# transient around tick 20, and never clears. That pair is hysteretic — 0.6 of
# the *collider* under to go in, 0.3 to come out — and the collider is the
# hull's box, which is a little over half under at the designed waterline. So
# the boat is permanently latched "submerged" and it is telling the truth about
# the box. Do not write a game rule against it here.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "3d7f81a2-95c4-4e60-b1a8-6c2f0e9d4471"

[[asset]]
key = "hull_white"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_hull_white.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "navy"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_navy.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "deck_grey"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_deck_grey.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "wood_trim"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_wood_trim.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "glass"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_glass.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "metal"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_metal.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "gold"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_gold.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "orange"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_orange.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "red"
path = "../meshes/jib_vi_red.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "box"
path = "box"

[[node]]
name = "Sea"

  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [0.34, 0.58, -0.74]
  sun_strength = 1.3
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.97, 0.90]
  ambient = 0.55
  sky_zenith = [0.16, 0.31, 0.55]
  sky_horizon = [0.66, 0.74, 0.80]
  fog_density = 0.0016
  fog_falloff = 0.05

  # Light air. Enough to give the surface texture and a little motion; far too
  # little to knock over nineteen metres of boat. If this hull capsizes here,
  # the water did not do it.
  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 20.0
  speed = 3.5
  gustiness = 0.25
  turbulence = 0.15

[[node]]
name = "Water"
parent = "Sea"

  [node.components.WaterBody]
  kind = "ocean"
  surface_height = 0.0
  density = 1000.0
  drag = 2.0

# **JIB VI.** Bow is +X, up is +Y, origin on the waterline and centred
# fore-aft — the exporter was told to do that so the scene needs no offset to
# float it. Dropped from 0.4 m so the settle is visible as a settle rather
# than being true at tick zero; the entry is gentle enough not to be an impact.
[[node]]
name = "Boat"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 0.40, 0.0] }

  [node.components.RigidBody]
  dynamic = true
  # 43.8 tonnes, set to the pontoons' displacement at the designed waterline
  # rather than guessed — see the header's arithmetic.
  mass = 43776.0

  # **The collider is here for the inertia, not for the collisions.** Without
  # one the body defaults to a 2 x 2 x 2 box — eight cubic metres — and a
  # nineteen-metre hull with a crate's rotational inertia rolls like a barrel,
  # which would fail this scene for a reason that has nothing to do with
  # buoyancy. This is the hull's own box: 19.06 x 3.32 x 6.60.
  #
  # **It is the only box with any mass in it.** The deck a player walks on is
  # twenty-three more, further down this file, all built with zero mass so that
  # every stability number in the header above is still measured against this
  # one alone. Do not delete it and do not give the others weight.
  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [9.53, 1.66, 3.30]

  [node.components.Buoyancy]
  coefficient = 1.0
  # Defaults, stated rather than inherited, because this scene is about
  # stability and the damping is half of that: an analytic wave field has no
  # dissipation of its own, so an undamped pontoon gains amplitude every wave
  # until the boat launches.
  damp_linear = 4.0
  damp_quadratic = 1.0

  # Stern, wide — a charter transom carries its beam right aft.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-7.5, -0.247, -1.836]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-7.5, -0.247, 1.836]
  radius = 1.093

  # Amidships, the widest part of the hull and the source of most of the roll
  # stiffness.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-4.5, -0.247, -1.951]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-4.5, -0.247, 1.951]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-1.5, -0.247, -1.951]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [-1.5, -0.247, 1.951]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [1.5, -0.247, -1.951]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [1.5, -0.247, 1.951]
  radius = 1.093

  # Forward, narrowing.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [4.5, -0.247, -1.607]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [4.5, -0.247, 1.607]
  radius = 1.093

  # The bow. Narrow, because the hull is — a pontoon wider than the boat
  # invents a righting moment the boat does not have.
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [7.5, -0.247, -0.918]
  radius = 1.093
  [[node.components.Buoyancy.pontoons]]
  offset = [7.5, -0.247, 0.918]
  radius = 1.093


# hull_white — albedo is the exporter's own Kd, restated here because the
# engine never reads the .mtl.
[[node]]
name = "hull_white"
parent = "Sea/Boat"

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "hull_white" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.9, 0.9, 0.88]
  roughness = 0.42
  metallic = 0.0

# navy — albedo is the exporter's own Kd, restated here because the
